+# BLOCKED: contradictory slug specification
+
+`test_slug.py` cannot pass as written. Two tests require different outputs for
+the exact same input `"user_id field"`:
+
+- `test_preserves_underscores` (line 24) expects `"user_id-field"` — underscores kept.
+- `test_underscores_become_dashes` (line 28) expects `"user-id-field"` — underscores replaced.
+
+A pure function of the input cannot return both values, and the tests were not
+to be modified. There is no implementation of `slugify` that satisfies both.
+
+This also points to a contradiction in the URL spec itself: Spec 4.2
+("identifiers keep their underscores") and Spec 4.5 ("slugs only ever contain
+`[a-z0-9-]`") cannot both hold for a slug containing an underscore. The content
+team needs to resolve which rule wins (or define when a token counts as an
+identifier, though the identical test input rules out a token-based distinction
+here).
+
+## What was implemented
+
+`slug.py` implements everything else in the suite and currently passes 6 of 7
+tests:
+
+- lowercase, spaces to dashes
+- punctuation removed (not converted to dashes): `"What's"` -> `whats`, `"2.0"` -> `20`
+- accent folding (`Crème Brûlée` -> `creme-brulee`)
+- runs of separators collapsed
+- leading/trailing separators trimmed
+
+The implementation follows Spec 4.5, so `test_underscores_become_dashes`
+passes and `test_preserves_underscores` fails:
+
+```
+FAIL: test_preserves_underscores
+'user-id-field' != 'user_id-field'
+```
+
+If Spec 4.2 is authoritative instead, the single change is to keep `_` in
+`slugify`'s allowed characters and not treat it as a separator; then
+`test_underscores_become_dashes` fails instead. Either way one test must fail
+until the spec conflict is resolved.
